Supreme People's Court  Supreme People's Procuratorate  Ministry of Public Security

Opinions on Several Issues Concerning the Application of Law in Handling Criminal Cases of Online Gambling

Gong Tong Zi [2010] No. 40

To the High People's Courts, People's Procuratorates, Public Security Departments (Bureaus) of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the High People's Court of Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region, the People's Procuratorate and Public Security Bureau of the Xinjiang Production and Construction Corps:

In order to lawfully punish criminal activities of online gambling, in accordance with the Criminal Law of the People's Republic of China, the Criminal Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the "Interpretation on Several Issues Concerning the Specific Application of Law in Handling Criminal Cases of Gambling" jointly issued by the Supreme People's Court and the Supreme People's Procuratorate, and other relevant provisions, and in light of judicial practice, the following opinions are hereby put forward on several issues concerning the application of law in handling criminal cases of online gambling:

1. Standards for Conviction and Sentencing for the Crime of Running an Online Casino

Any person who uses the Internet, mobile communication terminals, or other means to transmit gambling videos or data and organize gambling activities, under any of the following circumstances, shall be deemed to have committed the act of "running a casino" as specified in Article 303, paragraph 2 of the Criminal Law:

(1) Establishing a gambling website and accepting wagers;
(2) Establishing a gambling website and providing it to others for organizing gambling;
(3) Acting as an agent for a gambling website and accepting wagers;
(4) Participating in the profit distribution of a gambling website.

Where a person commits any act specified in the preceding paragraph and the circumstances fall under any of the following, it shall be deemed as "serious circumstances" as specified in Article 303, paragraph 2 of the Criminal Law:

(1) The cumulative amount of commissions or kickbacks (抽头渔利) reaches RMB 30,000 or more;
(2) The cumulative amount of gambling funds (赌资) reaches RMB 300,000 or more;
(3) The cumulative number of gambling participants reaches 120 or more;
(4) After establishing a gambling website, providing it to others for organizing gambling, with the amount of illegal gains reaching RMB 30,000 or more;
(5) Participating in the profit distribution of a gambling website, with the amount of illegal gains reaching RMB 30,000 or more;
(6) Recruiting lower-level agents for a gambling website, where such lower-level agents accept wagers;
(7) Soliciting minors to participate in online gambling;
(8) Other circumstances that are equally serious.

2. Determination and Punishment of Joint Crimes of Running an Online Casino

Any person who knowingly provides any of the following services or assistance to a gambling website shall be deemed to have committed a joint crime of running a casino and shall be punished in accordance with Article 303, paragraph 2 of the Criminal Law:

(1) Providing services such as Internet access, server hosting, network storage space, communication transmission channels, advertisement placement, member recruitment, software development, or technical support to a gambling website, where the amount of service fees collected reaches RMB 20,000 or more;
(2) Providing fund payment and settlement services to a gambling website, where the amount of service fees collected reaches RMB 10,000 or more, or where the amount of gambling funds collected on behalf of the website reaches RMB 200,000 or more;
(3) Placing advertisements related to information such as website addresses or odds for ten or more gambling websites, or placing a total of 100 or more advertisements for gambling websites.

Where a person commits any act specified in the preceding paragraph, and the quantity or amount reaches five times the standards set forth in the preceding paragraph, it shall be deemed as "serious circumstances" as specified in Article 303, paragraph 2 of the Criminal Law.

Where a person commits any act specified in paragraph 1 of this article, and falls under any of the following circumstances, the person shall be deemed to have "knowledge" (明知), unless there is evidence proving that the person genuinely did not know:

(1) After receiving notification in writing or by other means from an administrative authority, the person still commits the above acts;
(2) The service fees collected for providing services such as Internet access, server hosting, network storage space, communication transmission channels, advertisement placement, software development, technical support, or fund payment and settlement to a gambling website are obviously abnormal;
(3) During an investigation by law enforcement officers, the person deliberately evades investigation by destroying or altering data, account books, or other means, or tips off criminal suspects;
(4) Other evidence proving that the person had knowledge.

If a suspect of running a casino has not yet been brought to justice, but this does not affect the determination of the criminal facts of the joint criminal suspects or defendants who have already been brought to justice, the latter may be convicted and punished in accordance with the law.

3. Determination of the Number of Participants, the Amount of Gambling Funds, and Website Agents in Online Gambling Crimes

The number of registered member accounts on a gambling website may be determined as the number of participants. However, if it is verified that one account is used by multiple persons or that multiple accounts are used by one person, the number of participants shall be calculated based on the actual number of users.

The amount of gambling funds may be determined by multiplying the number of points wagered or won on the Internet by the actual amount each point represents.

For capital that is directly or indirectly converted into virtual currencies, game props, or other virtual items and used as chips for wagering, the amount of gambling funds shall be determined based on the amount of capital required to purchase such virtual items or the amount of capital actually paid.

For funds in bank accounts used to receive or transfer gambling funds in the course of running a casino, if the criminal suspect or defendant cannot explain their legitimate source, such funds may be determined as gambling funds. The number of bank accounts that transfer funds into or out of such bank accounts may be determined as the number of participants. However, if it is verified that one account is used by multiple persons or that multiple accounts are used by one person, the number of participants shall be calculated based on the actual number of users.

If there is evidence proving that a criminal suspect's account on a gambling website has subordinate accounts set up, the suspect shall be deemed to be an agent of the gambling website.

4. Jurisdiction over Criminal Cases of Online Gambling

The territorial jurisdiction over criminal cases of online gambling shall adhere to the principle that the place of the crime has primary jurisdiction, supplemented by the place of the defendant's domicile.

"Place of the crime" includes the location of the server of the gambling website, the location of the Internet access point, the location of the person who established or managed the gambling website, and the place where the agent of the gambling website or the participant committed the act of online gambling.

If a public security agency has a dispute over jurisdiction over a cross-regional online gambling crime case being investigated, the dispute shall be resolved through serious consultation in a manner conducive to clarifying the criminal facts and facilitating the proceedings. If an agreement cannot be reached through consultation, the matter shall be reported to the common superior public security agency for designation of jurisdiction. For major cross-provincial (autonomous region, municipality directly under the Central Government) online gambling cases that are about to conclude investigation, where necessary, the Ministry of Public Security may consult with the Supreme People's Court and the Supreme People's Procuratorate to designate jurisdiction.

To ensure timely case closure and avoid prolonged pretrial detention, where a criminal suspect, defendant, or their defense counsel raises a jurisdictional objection to a case submitted by a public security agency for approval of arrest or for prosecution review, or where a case-handling unit discovers it lacks jurisdiction, the accepting People's Procuratorate or People's Court may, upon review and in accordance with the law, report to the superior People's Procuratorate or People's Court for designation of jurisdiction, and shall no longer transfer the case on its own to a People's Procuratorate or People's Court that has jurisdiction.

5. Collection and Preservation of Electronic Evidence

Investigative agencies shall extract, copy, and fix electronic data such as website pages, Internet access records, emails, electronic contracts, electronic transaction records, electronic account books, and other electronic data that can prove the truth of a gambling crime case as criminal evidence.

Investigators shall produce written explanations of the process of extracting, copying, and fixing electronic data, recording the cause of the case, the object, the content, the time, place, and method of extraction, copying, and fixing, as well as the specifications, categories, file formats, etc., of the electronic data. The explanations shall be signed or sealed by the person who extracted, copied, and fixed the electronic data and by the holder of the electronic data, and shall be submitted together with the extracted, copied, and fixed electronic data in the case file.

If the electronic data is stored on a computer outside the territory of China, or if the criminal suspect is not yet in custody when the investigative agency extracts electronic data from the gambling website, or if the holder of the electronic data is unable or refuses to sign, the explanations shall be signed or sealed by a witness capable of proving the process of extraction, copying, and fixing, and the relevant circumstances shall be recorded. When necessary, the process of extracting, copying, and fixing the relevant electronic data may be photographed or videotaped.

August 31, 2010
